{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"527fb13a00244023","type":"group","x":-5760,"y":-960,"width":2100,"height":200,"label":"Microscopic version"},
		{"id":"8163333b7c5fc839","type":"group","x":-5760,"y":-660,"width":2100,"height":180,"label":"Macroscopic version"},
		{"id":"df6f49546e91d9e9","type":"group","x":900,"y":1000,"width":320,"height":220,"label":"Types of Electric Force"},
		{
			"id":"8fe20b8b53734418",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Conduction Electrons== are free or delocalized electrons within a solid material that can move easily and carry an electric current.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-610,
			"y":-1015,
			"width":380,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"7b12c7b4bbf6c131",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Charge== is a fundamental physical property of matter that allows particles to experience and exert electric forces on one another.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-610,
			"y":-197,
			"width":380,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"a4d3cc9da1de1292",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical conductance and resistance are affected by a material's electron density (charge carrier density).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2100,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":445,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"2c61ffb3fd2cb36b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Drude's formula for electrical conductivity==\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\frac{ne^2 \\tau}{m}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $\\sigma$ = electrical conductivity\n- $n$ = free electron density\n- $e^2$ = elementary charge\n- $\\tau$ = relaxation time\n- $m$ = electron mass",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1106,
			"y":-1320,
			"width":371,
			"height":385
		},
		{
			"id":"62c8b8d1d97897a8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Relaxation Time== is the average time an electron can travel before colliding with an atom or defect. A higher relaxation time means electrons can travel further without bumping into obstacles - which results in higher conductance.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1240,
			"y":-840,
			"width":545,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"b88dfe684e96802d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"How Drude's formula is derived",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1720,
			"y":-840,
			"width":340,
			"height":90
		},
		{
			"id":"44a4fac076d12657",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A single charged particle is the source of an electric field $\\vec{E}$ that extends through space in all directions, regardless of whether a second charge is present.\n\nHaving a net charge dictates how a particle responds to and creates electromagnetic fields.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-635,
			"y":240,
			"width":435,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"22dab4c638565975",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = - \\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4560,
			"y":-905,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"b8ca0956f1396408",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_{0} \\left(\\vec{J} + \\varepsilon_{0} \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t}\\right)\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4130,
			"y":-910,
			"width":440,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"1fc47e21dacb6540",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = - \\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4560,
			"y":-630,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"8a6dfa2d63e1f768",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{H} = \\vec{J}_{f} + \\frac{\\partial \\vec{D}}{\\partial t}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4060,
			"y":-630,
			"width":300,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"0cc69427ab37530b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Where:\n- $\\vec{E}$ = electric field\n- $\\vec{B}$ = magnetic field\n- $\\rho$ = total electric charge density\n- $\\vec{J}$ = total current density\n- $\\mu_{0}$ = vacuum permeability\n- $\\nabla \\cdot$ = divergence operator\n- $\\nabla \\times$ = curl operator",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6360,
			"y":-710,
			"width":440,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"d2c3f618c83003f9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Where:\n- $\\vec{E}$ = electric field\n- $\\vec{B}$ = magnetic field\n- $\\vec{D}$ = electric displacement field\n- $\\vec{H}$ = magnetic field intensity\n- $\\rho_{f}$ = free electric charge density\n- $\\vec{J}_{f}$ = free current density\n- $\\nabla \\cdot$ = divergence operator\n- $\\nabla \\times$ = curl operator",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":-805,
			"width":440,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"5aeefa3be756cd2b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{H} = \\vec{J}_{f}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2840,
			"y":-855,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"6bef558fb3f504db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Ampere's Law==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2830,
			"y":-980,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"70402503c4844ca7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical conductivity $\\sigma$ is directly proportional to the number of free electrons per unit volume $n$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1532,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":345,
			"height":145
		},
		{
			"id":"a02d8b5e2e64ffaa",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{B} = 0\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5080,
			"y":-895,
			"width":260,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"40198a8110338736",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E} = \\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_{0}}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5640,
			"y":-905,
			"width":260,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"e73897abe31c9767",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{B} = 0\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5080,
			"y":-620,
			"width":260,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"f9da1d85385f8c81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{D} = \\rho_{f}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5650,
			"y":-620,
			"width":280,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"3a4ee7664b023957",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Faraday's Law of Induction==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4580,
			"y":-1380,
			"width":320,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"29163aad136c0907",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A magnetic field that changes over time induces a rotating (curling) electric field",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4580,
			"y":-1240,
			"width":320,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"7901c1422ae92a5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==The Ampere-Maxwell Law==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4100,
			"y":-1400,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"e09403b9c5622675",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical resistance is the mathematical reciprocal and opposite of electrical conductance. Meaning, they are inversely proportional.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-550,
			"y":-1600,
			"width":460,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"3f974354b2f5140a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\nG = \\frac{1}{R}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-905,
			"y":-1540,
			"width":170,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"53a210b2fff33b6e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical resistance is like microscopic friction caused by metal's fixed atoms and ions in the crystal lattice. Changing the crystal lattice arrangement alters the metal's resistance.\n\nIn quantum mechanics, electrons travel through a *perfectly* arranged crystal lattice as smoothly as a wave passing through clear water. What actually causes electrical resistance is not the atoms themselves, but deviations or disruptions in that perfect lattice which scatter the electrons.\n\nMechanical stress (by bend, roll, or hammering a cold metal) multiplies the number of grain boundaries (the borders where misaligned crystal structures crash into each other). Electrons constantly smash into these boundaries, causing electrical resistance to increase.\n\nAnnealing (baking a heavily stressed, high-resistance metal at a high temperature and then letting it cool slowly) wipes away the grain boundaries, effectively resetting the chaotic crystal lattice arrangement back into a large, immaculate structure. Therefore, it decreases the metal's resistance.\n\nSome metals completely switch their geometric crystal patterns at different temperatures. These allotropes have different levels of resistivity. For example, iron at room temperature has a Body-Centered Cubic (BCC) structure, but when heated past 912 degree Celsius, it rearranges its atoms into a Face-Centered Cubic (FCC) structure.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2145,
			"y":-2480,
			"width":730,
			"height":570
		},
		{
			"id":"7c736e7f825e76a1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-680,
			"y":-2140,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f92fcf389ab853e8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"ohm $\\Omega$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1010,
			"y":-2300,
			"width":130,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"e152e084a86a07d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical conductance and resistance are determined by a material's electronic structure; specifically, the availability and mobility of free electrons that can move around to carry an electric current.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2280,
			"y":-1490,
			"width":660,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"60755f6ec6460b2a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electromagnetism==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4900,
			"y":-2040,
			"width":340,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"41f988c7269b764c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Foundation of classical electromagnetism",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4880,
			"y":-1900,
			"width":300,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"91cb8c1c01ece953",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Four Maxwell's Equations (Differential Form)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4960,
			"y":-1720,
			"width":460,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"9f4c2d60508c5839","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260926203057.jpg","x":-5680,"y":-2296,"width":543,"height":673},
		{
			"id":"d3d6195ec4782a68",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Magnetic fields are generated either by electric current density ($J$) or by an electric field changing over time ($\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial t}$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4100,
			"y":-1260,
			"width":380,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"c648267ecc629c0d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Furthermore, it showed that self-sustaining oscillating electric and magnetic fields propagate through space at the speed of light, proving light itself is an electromagnetic force.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3820,
			"y":-1755,
			"width":600,
			"height":132
		},
		{
			"id":"fc8f9ce3ed5c649c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"These equations proved that electricity and magnetism are two aspects of a single fundamental force (electromagnetism).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4390,
			"y":-1747,
			"width":480,
			"height":117
		},
		{
			"id":"951bfea00d0bf046",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Allotropes== are different structural forms of the same chemical element in the same physical state (e.g. carbon -> graphite and diamond). Even though they consist of the exact same type of atoms, allotropes have different atomic arrangements or bonding patterns. Because of this structural difference, they often exhibit drastically different physical and chemical properties.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2980,
			"y":-2140,
			"width":655,
			"height":220
		},
		{"id":"06ef4b3d449ce793","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260927111109.jpg","x":-3028,"y":-2480,"width":752,"height":325},
		{
			"id":"e3d863f8af84331b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Conductor== is an object or type of material that allows the flow of charge (electric current) in one or more directions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2980,
			"y":-1480,
			"width":360,
			"height":150
		},
		{
			"id":"6f1de6fe793c31fd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Magnetic monopoles do not exist. Magnetic field lines are continuous loops; the total magnetic flux out of any closed surface is always zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5150,
			"y":-1240,
			"width":400,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"ca7ba63efaafb7e1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Gauss's Law of Magnetic Fields==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5120,
			"y":-1380,
			"width":340,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"6840973c75f7e11c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electric charges create diverging electric fields. Net electric flux out of a closed surface is proportional to the enclosed electric charge density ($\\rho$) divided by the permittivity of free space ($\\varepsilon_{0}$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5760,
			"y":-1260,
			"width":480,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"6bd7583578235311",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Gauss's Law for Electric Fields==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5660,
			"y":-1380,
			"width":380,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"367ea13768e018c6","type":"file","file":"attachments/del operator (nabla).jpg","x":-6700,"y":-1560,"width":720,"height":720},
		{
			"id":"f12af2c97b0026cb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Microscopic version",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5640,
			"y":-1100,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"8883e19e8d2b0786","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260927122156.jpg","x":-170,"y":2700,"width":1160,"height":653},
		{
			"id":"3d550ec7e657e6e0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":645,
			"y":2180,
			"width":420,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"17e8aced77633015",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Field== is an invisible region surrounding a charged particle or object where other electric charges experience a non-contact force (electric force).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":15,
			"y":1900,
			"width":490,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"84bd6fa0ef41063c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The electric field $E$ is defined mathematically as the electric force $F$ exerted on a small, positive test charge $q$ divided by the magnitude of that charge.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":625,
			"y":1900,
			"width":415,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"12796cb50fb09bd2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Charge can neither be created nor destroyed; it can only be transferred from place to place, from one object to another.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1160,
			"y":560,
			"width":510,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"c401bc214a8e93ec",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"If two objects that have equal and opposite charges are physically close to each other, then the forces they apply on some other charged object cancel, for a net force of zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1160,
			"y":720,
			"width":510,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"13254e8293498b51",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The smallest charge is $e \\equiv 1.602 \\times 10^{-19} \\,\\text{C}$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1160,
			"y":170,
			"width":420,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"2b76c31c1e63b8ef",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Charge is a scalar quantity, meaning it has magnitude but no direction. The positive/negative indication only refers to algebraic polarity - they are opposite states that can cancel each other out to equal zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1340,
			"y":320,
			"width":480,
			"height":180
		},
		{"id":"36261970f8eaa98f","type":"file","file":"attachments/EF_negative_charge.svg","x":1790,"y":2460,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"cb008f77cfeb2af3","type":"file","file":"attachments/EF_positive_charge.svg","x":1350,"y":2880,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"affb20b9b6e2df5f","type":"file","file":"attachments/electric_field_of_dipole.svg","x":1230,"y":2510,"width":400,"height":300},
		{
			"id":"81a849551b7830f5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Newtons per Coulomb $N/C$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1215,
			"y":2120,
			"width":320,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"6d7604ebf9dbfd6b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Volts per Meter $V/m$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1215,
			"y":2240,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"bfb2ae912fca2c6a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$E = \\frac{F}{q}$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1145,
			"y":1950,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"3c34323da40e6145",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly represented as rho $\\rho$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":670,
			"y":-1300,
			"width":340,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"6498f3d412ef4d34",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\rho = \\frac{E}{J}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n- E = electric field\n- J = current density",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":-1890,
			"width":260,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"01cd9bb1a7b36d91",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Microscopic formula",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":970,
			"y":-1800,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1edff7e5c97bef1e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The formula for the resistance/resistivity of a uniform conductor",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":855,
			"y":-2210,
			"width":395,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"cb9e1df0e7fbcc60",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":760,
			"y":-1440,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"fe8e161093bc0633",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Ohm-meter $\\ohm \\cdot m$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1120,
			"y":-1440,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"0f5cd398a807c979",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\rho = R \\cdot \\frac{A}{L}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n- R = electrical resistance in ohms\n- A = cross-sectional area in square meters\n- L = length of the material in meters",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":-2320,
			"width":390,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"abf0e45514274857",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Negative Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":540,
			"y":-360,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"e1ebd7b10a5b64fb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Positive Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":540,
			"y":-160,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"99e0671db9687d73",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Coulombs $C$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":13,
			"width":210,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"5022d859808c225a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly represented as sigma $\\sigma$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":670,
			"y":-1030,
			"width":340,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"dbfa2967838af430",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Conductivity can be thought of as a measure of how easily the conduction electrons move through a material.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":640,
			"y":-710,
			"width":340,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"1c17c6751639d046",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":730,
			"y":-860,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"8999aa1ccdb5bd5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"(SI unit) Siemens per Meter $S/m$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1120,
			"y":-900,
			"width":360,
			"height":70
		},
		{
			"id":"c33f7d91fee0e588",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\sigma = \\frac{J}{E}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1840,
			"y":-1220,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"334ec36ab747821d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\sigma = \\frac{1}{\\rho}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1420,
			"y":-1235,
			"width":240,
			"height":130
		},
		{"id":"436e65106e708188","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260926195315.jpg","x":1120,"y":-731,"width":516,"height":343},
		{
			"id":"af4ba51e8a2a9d48",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Properties of Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":170,
			"y":260,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"fe950b9e623c28b0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Charge is quantized.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":240,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1217f94900cf2ebb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Law of Conservation of Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":580,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"f8e1d274febf81cb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The magnitude of the charge is independent of the type.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":400,
			"width":520,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"65ab50771e2ed9e4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":175,
			"y":8,
			"width":245,
			"height":65
		},
		{
			"id":"3ce8dda98aa2538e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Conductivity== is a material's intrinsic property to conduct an electric current.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":30,
			"y":-1060,
			"width":460,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"f031b6825ba0b607",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Conduction electrons can be thought of as physical vehicles that carry the electric charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":100,
			"y":-700,
			"width":320,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"35a903218240f8a0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Force== is the attractive or repulsive push and pull that exists between any two charged particles or objects.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":290,
			"y":1060,
			"width":260,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"20475392f008a131",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Types of Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":180,
			"y":-240,
			"width":240,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"c3e9d7497076e16a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Resistivity== is a material's intrinsic, fundamental property of a specific material itself. It measures how strongly a substance resists current regardless of its shape or size.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":30,
			"y":-1345,
			"width":460,
			"height":150
		},
		{
			"id":"a1e25a31ef08aab3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Resistance== measures how much a specific object opposes the flow of electric current. It depends on the material and the physical dimensions (length and thickness) of the object.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-270,
			"y":-1940,
			"width":600,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"f74303dd64e81090",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Ohm's Law== states that the electric current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage applied across it, provided its temperature remains constant.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-180,
			"y":-2780,
			"width":460,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"6bdc22a94edf026b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\nV = IR\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-40,
			"y":-2520,
			"width":180,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"7ae7aeffe501e63c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrostatic Repulsion",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":925,
			"y":1040,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"418dd8da8350ec78",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrostatic Attraction",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":925,
			"y":1120,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"335f6ee62aaa8b64",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly denoted as $F$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":905,
			"y":1300,
			"width":280,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a7c86e0c59516d34",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"An electric force requires an interaction between at least two charged objects ($q_{1}$ and $q_{2}$). A single isolated particle cannot exert a force on itself or \"have\" a force waiting to happen in empty space.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":200,
			"y":1380,
			"width":440,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"9d9a90433f2fcc65",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electric force $\\vec{F}$ arises locally; an electric force only materializes when a second, \"test\" charge $q$ enters an electric field $\\vec{E}$ and interacts with the field right at its own location.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-65,
			"y":480,
			"width":430,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"20c9f7c186a3db93",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\vec{F}=q\\vec{E}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":60,
			"y":740,
			"width":180,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"d3a1f1530dbf0f81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Current== is the rate of flow of electric charge past a specific point in a conductor or circuit.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2760,
			"y":-207,
			"width":440,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"a3e7afa47898ec9e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"1 amp equals 1 coulomb of charge moving past a point per second.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3920,
			"y":-187,
			"width":280,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"52ba99657dc187c9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Current Density== specifies precisely where and in which direction the charge flows at any given point in space.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":-515,
			"width":420,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"243e4024a9cdaefc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Amperes (amps) $A$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":-157,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f819c013e7e06aa0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3160,
			"y":-157,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f02343275f6b3c04",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electric current requires a voltage source (electrical pressure) and a complete, closed circuit loop to flow.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3540,
			"y":50,
			"width":360,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"8a97b3b31918dac5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly represented as $I$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":270,
			"width":320,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"b55b4f05b95c6bfd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Direct Current (DC)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3240,
			"y":650,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"efaf4fade959011d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alternating Current (AC)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2820,
			"y":710,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"243542db4a70c655",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electromagnetic field can be described as either global or local.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1320,
			"y":720,
			"width":420,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"2bdcc77491550e28",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the *classical electrodynamics*, it is called the **background field** or **medium**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1720,
			"y":1170,
			"width":425,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"b2a8a94540481bd0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the *quantum field theory*, it is called **unexcited field** or the **vacuum state**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2160,
			"y":1170,
			"width":340,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"e24a46f7d52ab882",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The \"Global\" Field is the infinite, underlying fabric of the universe when it is at its lowest possible energy level. It fills all space and time but has an average value of zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2030,
			"y":950,
			"width":560,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"a488ba8d89bfeeee",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the quantum field theory, it is called a **field excitation** or **quantum**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1160,
			"y":1220,
			"width":380,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"e3f54ee7041f4cc2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The \"Local\" Field is the localized disturbance/excitation/ripple that is generated by a particle.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-900,
			"y":950,
			"width":380,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"1c48ad5d1ea7c6dc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the classical electrodynamics, it is divided into **Near-Fields** and **Far-Fields**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-710,
			"y":1200,
			"width":320,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"d2b5f188376d3444",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Near-Field is the localized, non-propagating cloud of electromagnetic energy immediately surrounding a charged particle or a magnet.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-900,
			"y":1480,
			"width":380,
			"height":200
		}
	],
